WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.860
How do we measure computational

2
00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:05.260
complexity and compare growth? Complexity

3
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.200
theory groups decision languages by

4
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.880
the resources required by some

5
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.320
machine deciding them. We will

6
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:14.180
distinguish input length, worst-case runtime

7
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.720
and asymptotic bounds before simplifying

8
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.940
the tutorial’s functions.

9
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.260
Big O is an eventual

10
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.540
upper bound up to a

11
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.720
fixed constant, Omega is the

12
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.780
corresponding lower bound, and Theta

13
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.200
requires both. State the constants

14
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.280
and sufficiently large input threshold

15
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.620
when proving a bound from

16
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.880
definitions. Saying that an algorithm

17
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.480
is at most quadratic does

18
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.220
not establish a tight quadratic

19
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:37.820
runtime.

20
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.300
For a polynomial with positive

21
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.520
leading coefficient, the highest degree

22
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.980
dominates asymptotically. Fixed log-base changes

23
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.180
multiply by a constant, while

24
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.580
two to c log base

25
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.020
two n equals n to

26
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.020
c. Thus an exponential-looking expression

27
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.780
can simplify to a polynomial

28
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.000
when its exponent is logarithmic.

29
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.340
The expression two to log

30
00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.920
base two n squared means

31
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.560
two raised to the square

32
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.060
of that logarithm. It equals

33
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.520
n raised to log base

34
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.660
two n, whose exponent grows

35
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:09.940
with n. That is superpolynomial

36
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.080
but slower than a fixed-base

37
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.580
exponential such as three to

38
00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:13.760
n.

39
00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.480
The tutorial growth ordering places

40
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.260
square root n before linear

41
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.500
n, then n square root

42
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.280
log n, n log n

43
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.000
and cubic n. The logarithmic-exponent

44
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.520
function follows every fixed polynomial,

45
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.280
with three to n growing

46
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.940
faster still. Constants such as

47
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.760
seven n minus two do

48
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.340
not change the linear class.

49
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.020
Do not exponentiate a Big

50
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.780
O relation as though constants

51
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.440
disappear inside an exponent. Let

52
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:43.920
f equal two n and

53
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.740
g equal n, so f

54
00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.400
is Big O of g.

55
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.460
Yet two to f divided

56
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:50.920
by two to g equals

57
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.440
two to n, which is

58
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.480
unbounded, disproving the proposed exponentiated

59
00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:55.800
bound.

60
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.000
Average case also needs a

61
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.380
stated distribution. If a linear-search

62
00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.200
target is equally likely at

63
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.520
any of n positions or

64
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.660
absent, sum costs one through

65
00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.200
n plus another n for

66
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.280
absence, then divide by n

67
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.400
plus one. The exact expectation

68
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.900
is n times n plus

69
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.520
three over twice n plus

70
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:15.840
one, which is Theta n.

71
00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.580
The printed solution’s simplified n

72
00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.240
plus two over two expression

73
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.380
is not that exact expectation.

74
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.540
Checking n equal to one

75
00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.740
exposes the issue immediately, because

76
00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.780
every possible search costs one

77
00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.660
comparison. The asymptotic linear conclusion

78
00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.220
survives, but an exact numerical

79
00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.420
claim still needs correct algebra.

80
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.700
Machine-model changes can introduce overhead,

81
00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.220
such as quadratic simulation of

82
00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.900
standard multitape time by a

83
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.640
single tape under suitable bounds.

84
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.980
Time-hierarchy theorems give strict separations

85
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.040
when their constructibility and growth-gap

86
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.220
hypotheses hold. They do not

87
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.740
let us declare P different

88
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.280
from NP, and sublinear examples

89
00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.860
need careful model assumptions.

90
00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:00.820
Things to take note of.

91
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.540
Simplify functions symbolically, separate upper

92
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.520
bounds from tight bounds, and

93
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.960
count time as a function

94
00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.280
of encoded input length. State

95
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.060
the distribution for averages and

96
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.620
the model assumptions for hierarchy

97
00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.400
or simulation arguments; memorizing a

98
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.780
growth table is not a

99
00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.160
substitute for those conditions.
